o the composite electrode leads to the reduction in XRD peak intensity due to several structural and physical reasons: i) X-ray attenuation: the polymer coating on top absorbs or scatter incoming and outgoing X-rays, reducing the number of photons that reach the detector from the material underneath; and ii) Lower electron density: the polymer layer consist of light elements (C and O) and its low electron density leads to much less efficiently scattering of X-rays than crystalline materials, resulting in lower peak heights [ 35 ,  36 ]. Overall, those findings confirmed the composite nature of the supercapacitor electrodes.

